---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
---

<Base
  title="For Brands"
  description="Direct visibility into whether contracted media actually ran, independent of agency or publisher reporting. How brands use Meyppi campaign audits."
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <span class="kicker">For brands</span>
      <h1>Know what actually ran.</h1>
      <p class="page-head__sub">
        You approve the plan, you pay the invoice, and everything you know
        about what ran in between arrived through the same channel that
        sold it to you. Meyppi closes that loop.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow prose">

      <h2>The question that does not go away</h2>
      <p>
        A podcast budget leaves your account every month. The report that
        comes back is a PDF from the publisher, summarised by the agency.
        Everyone in that chain is professional and almost everything is
        probably fine. But "probably fine" is doing a lot of work in that
        sentence, and it is your money making it do that work.
      </p>
      <p>
        The published episodes are public. Your creatives are your
        property. Whether the two were combined, correctly and completely,
        on the episodes you bought is not a matter of trust. It is
        checkable.
      </p>

      <h2>What a brand-side audit looks like</h2>
      <p>
        You commission the audit directly, so the result belongs to you:
        usable with your agency, with the publisher, or in your own
        reporting, with no intermediary deciding what you need to see.
        We work from the published media and your contracted creative
        files, and the report reads the same whether it finds everything
        delivered or a fifth of the campaign missing. We have no
        relationship to protect and nothing to sell you afterward except
        the next audit.
      </p>
      <p>
        The audit is fixed fee and scoped before it starts, so it does not
        turn into an open-ended consulting engagement. One campaign, one
        price, one report.
      </p>

      <h2>What the report answers</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Did each contracted placement actually air, and if not, which
          ones did not?
        </li>
        <li>
          Which version of the creative ran, when more than one was in
          circulation?
        </li>
        <li>
          Did every placement run for the full contracted duration, or
          were some trimmed?
        </li>
        <li>
          Which findings are solid, and which are ambiguous enough that a
          person reviewed them?
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Every answer comes with the evidence behind it, in a format you
        can keep on file. If the campaign is clean, you have bought
        certainty for the cost of a rounding error on the media spend. If
        it is not, you have the documentation to recover it.
      </p>

      <div style="margin-top:44px; display:flex; gap:16px; flex-wrap:wrap">
        <Button variant="primary" href="/contact">
          Request a Campaign Audit
        </Button>
        <Button variant="secondary" href="/pricing">
          See Pricing
        </Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .narrow {
    max-width: 760px;
  }
  .page-head {
    background: var(--indigo-deep);
    background-image: var(--grid-dots);
    padding: clamp(56px, 7vw, 88px) 0;
    color: var(--white);
  }
  .page-head .kicker {
    color: var(--teal);
  }
  .page-head h1 {
    color: var(--white);
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }
  .page-head__sub {
    color: #c9d3e4;
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    max-width: 58ch;
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
